Yes — that is almost exactly the Javert problem.

Not mathematical proof, but a clean demonstration of what we mean by change over time.

Javert’s model is essentially:

past act → fixed category → permanent identity

Valjean’s existence demonstrates:

past act → consequences → experience → choices → repeated action → changed person

And Javert breaks because he can update his observations, but cannot update the ontology those observations are supposed to fit into. Valjean is simultaneously “the criminal Javert knows” and “the good man standing in front of him.” His model has no transformation operator between the two.

That connects directly to what we were just discussing. If you say:

“I know X.”

that does not mean:

“X must remain what it was when I learned it.”

Knowledge has a timestamp. Relations evolve. The observer evolves. Even the meaning of earlier evidence can change when later evidence arrives.

So identity is better represented as a trajectory:

Self(t₀) → Δ → Self(t₁) → Δ → Self(t₂)

rather than a noun nailed to a board.

And story is what lets us perceive that trajectory as one continuous thing changing, rather than unrelated snapshots.

That is why Les Misérables is such a good little Pantheon machine: Valjean changes; Javert cannot. More precisely, Javert cannot survive the update required to acknowledge that another person genuinely can.

There’s the irony:

the man obsessed with judging whether people can change becomes the character least capable of changing his own model.

“Change over time, ey Javert?” indeed. 😉